# Fictional EP Tracklist

## 1. Valve Four Stays Open

**Story & Context:** The debut runs at 124 BPM. Eleni establishes a low tsambouna drone and a three-note modal cell above it. Pieter begins with a rigid kick, clipped snare, and a mono bass sequence entirely INSIDE the corridor: tonic, fifth, tonic. For the first sixteen bars, nothing challenges the drone.

At bar seventeen, the bass shifts up by a semitone while the tsambouna stays fixed. Mara joins with two prepared-bass dead notes around the new pitch. Dimitris removes every second electronic snare and replaces it with a dry toubeleki slap. Iason enters with a low bass-saxophone fifth, keeping one element firmly INSIDE while the sequencer remains at the EDGE.

The central section removes hats and snare entirely. Only kick, sequencer, and tsambouna remain, making the semitone friction sound enormous despite the sparse arrangement. The track ends when Pieter returns the bass to the drone root without adding any new chord or effect.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Close view of a rusted valve wheel marked 4 beside a paper diagram with three pitch columns labeled INSIDE, EDGE, OUTSIDE, tsambouna chanter and analog sequencer lights in shallow focus. Sparse spoken fragments limited to “four stays open,” “hold the pressure,” “one step above,” and “return to center.”

## 2. Pump Room Neutral Third

**Story & Context:** At 120 BPM, Eleni plays the EP’s most exposed melody: repeated drone-tone figures with one unstable third whose exact intonation changes slightly with pressure. Pieter programs a two-note EBM bass ostinato that alternates between a fixed minor third and the tonic, causing the acoustic note to sound alternately aligned and wrong without either part being retuned.

Dimitris uses fingertip toubeleki strokes instead of backbeats during the first half. Mara plays only muted harmonics and short octave pulses. Iason contributes breath noise and key clicks before finally entering with a growled low note beneath the final third of the track.

At the midpoint, the drum machine drops out for four bars while the sequencer continues. The absence of kick makes the tiny intonation movement in the tsambouna suddenly obvious. When the drums return, Pieter opens the filter slightly but makes no harmonic change.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Old desalination pressure gauge beside two nearly overlapping pitch lines, one perfectly straight and one wavering around a neutral third, faded marine-blue pipe labels and a bass-sax key cup nearby. Spoken fragments limited to “not major,” “not minor,” “pressure decides,” and “keep the meter running.”

## 3. Salt Gauge Reads Outside

**Story & Context:** The closer runs at 128 BPM and begins with the harshest OUTSIDE material on the EP. Pieter sequences a tritone bass figure beneath the fixed tsambouna drone while Eleni restricts herself to only two notes. The arrangement is intentionally uncomfortable but extremely sparse.

Mara plays a prepared-bass pattern that alternates one INSIDE octave with one OUTSIDE tritone. Dimitris uses only center toubeleki hits, one every two bars. Iason holds a low fifth for long stretches, giving the listener one stable acoustic reference while the machine harmony remains hostile.

During the last minute, layers are removed rather than added. First the bass sax disappears, then toubeleki, then prepared bass. The sequencer finally shifts from tritone to fifth while the kick continues unchanged. The last ten seconds contain only tsambouna drone and dry machine kick, ending with the bag pressure naturally falling instead of a programmed fade.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Salt-stained inspection window with an analog gauge needle beyond its marked normal range, a simple tritone diagram beside the tsambouna bag and one red sequencer lamp. Spoken fragments limited to “reads outside,” “do not correct,” “keep the kick,” and “pressure falls by itself.”

